var e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"ESSAY-001","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["education","technology"],"prompt":"Some people believe that artificial intelligence will eventually replace human teachers in the classroom. Others argue that technology can never fully replace the human element in education. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence has sparked debate about the future role of human teachers in education. While AI offers certain advantages in personalizing learning and automating administrative tasks, I believe that human teachers will remain irreplaceable due to their unique ability to provide emotional support, mentorship, and adaptive instruction.\\n\\nProponents of AI in education point to its ability to provide personalized learning paths for each student. AI-powered platforms can analyze student performance data to identify knowledge gaps and deliver tailored exercises. Furthermore, AI can handle repetitive tasks such as grading multiple-choice assessments and tracking attendance, freeing up teachers to focus on more meaningful educational activities.\\n\\nHowever, education extends far beyond the transmission of information. Human teachers serve as mentors, role models, and sources of emotional support for their students. They can detect when a student is struggling emotionally, provide encouragement during difficult times, and celebrate achievements in a way that no algorithm can replicate. The classroom environment is fundamentally a social space where students learn communication, collaboration, and empathy through human interaction.\\n\\nIn my opinion, the ideal educational model combines the strengths of both approaches. AI tools can enhance teachers' capabilities by providing data-driven insights and automating routine tasks, while human teachers continue to provide the emotional connection, inspiration, and nuanced guidance that technology cannot replicate. Rather than replacing teachers, AI should be viewed as a powerful tool that supports and amplifies their effectiveness.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while AI will undoubtedly transform education, it cannot replace the deeply human elements of teaching. The future of education lies in harnessing technology to empower teachers, not to replace them.","keyArguments":["AI personalization","automation of tasks","human emotional support","mentorship role","combined approach"]},{"id":"ESSAY-002","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["environment","society"],"prompt":"Climate change is one of the most pressing issues of our time. Some argue that individual actions such as recycling and reducing energy consumption are insufficient, and that only government regulations and corporate accountability can make a meaningful difference. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Climate change presents an existential threat that demands action at every level of society. While I agree that government regulations and corporate accountability are essential, I disagree with the notion that individual actions are insufficient — both approaches must work in tandem to achieve meaningful results.\\n\\nGovernment regulations provide the legal framework necessary for large-scale environmental protection. Policies such as carbon pricing, emissions standards, and renewable energy mandates can drive systemic change across entire industries. Without such regulations, corporations have little incentive to prioritize environmental concerns over profit margins, as voluntary commitments have historically proven unreliable.\\n\\nHowever, individual actions should not be dismissed as inconsequential. Consumer choices send powerful market signals that influence corporate behavior. When millions of people choose sustainable products, reduce their energy consumption, and adopt environmentally friendly habits, the cumulative impact is substantial. Moreover, individuals who practice environmental responsibility often become advocates who vote for green policies and hold corporations accountable.\\n\\nIn my view, the most effective approach integrates both strategies. Governments must establish robust environmental regulations and enforce compliance, while corporations must innovate to reduce their carbon footprint. Simultaneously, individuals must embrace sustainable lifestyles and use their power as consumers and citizens to drive change. This multi-pronged strategy creates a reinforcing cycle where each level of action amplifies the others.\\n\\nUltimately, framing climate action as a choice between individual and systemic change creates a false dichotomy. The scale of the climate crisis demands that we pursue every available avenue simultaneously.","keyArguments":["government regulation necessity","corporate accountability","consumer market influence","cumulative individual impact","integrated multi-level approach"]},{"id":"ESSAY-003","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["health","technology"],"prompt":"Telemedicine has become increasingly popular, especially after the global pandemic. Some people believe it is a convenient and cost-effective alternative to traditional in-person medical consultations, while others worry about misdiagnosis and the loss of the doctor-patient relationship. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The rapid adoption of telemedicine has transformed healthcare delivery, bringing both significant benefits and legitimate concerns. In my opinion, telemedicine represents a valuable complement to traditional care rather than a complete replacement for in-person consultations.\\n\\nTelemedicine offers undeniable advantages in terms of accessibility and efficiency. Patients in remote or underserved areas can consult specialists without traveling long distances, while those with mobility issues or chronic conditions benefit from reduced clinic visits. Additionally, telemedicine reduces waiting times and lowers healthcare costs for both patients and providers, making routine consultations more efficient for all parties involved.\\n\\nHowever, critics raise valid concerns about the limitations of remote diagnosis. Certain conditions require physical examination, diagnostic tests, or hands-on procedures that cannot be replicated through a screen. There is a genuine risk of misdiagnosis when doctors cannot observe subtle physical signs or conduct necessary tests. Furthermore, the intangible elements of the doctor-patient relationship — empathy, trust, and non-verbal communication — may be diminished in a virtual setting.\\n\\nI believe the optimal approach is a hybrid model that leverages telemedicine for appropriate cases while preserving in-person care when necessary. Routine follow-ups, mental health consultations, and initial triage are well-suited to virtual platforms. Meanwhile, complex diagnostics, emergency care, and situations requiring physical examination should remain in-person. This balanced approach maximizes the benefits of technology without compromising the quality of care.\\n\\nIn conclusion, telemedicine should be embraced as a powerful tool that extends the reach of healthcare, but it must be thoughtfully integrated alongside traditional medical practice.","keyArguments":["increased accessibility","cost and time efficiency","diagnostic limitations","loss of personal connection","hybrid healthcare model"]},{"id":"ESSAY-004","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["education","society"],"prompt":"Many countries are debating whether university education should be free for all citizens. Proponents argue that free education promotes equality and economic growth, while opponents claim it places an unfair burden on taxpayers. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The debate over free university education touches on fundamental questions about equality, economic policy, and the role of government. After considering both perspectives, I believe that a partially subsidized model with income-based repayment offers the most balanced solution.\\n\\nAdvocates of free university education rightly argue that it promotes social mobility and economic competitiveness. When higher education is accessible regardless of financial background, talented individuals from all socioeconomic levels can contribute their skills to the economy. Countries with affordable higher education, such as Germany and the Nordic nations, demonstrate that an educated workforce drives innovation and long-term economic growth. Furthermore, free education reduces the burden of student debt that can delay major life milestones for young graduates.\\n\\nOn the other hand, opponents raise legitimate concerns about the financial sustainability of fully subsidized higher education. Funding free university for all citizens requires significant public expenditure, which may lead to higher taxes or reduced spending in other critical areas such as healthcare and infrastructure. Additionally, when education is free, some students may lack the motivation to complete their degrees efficiently, potentially leading to wasted resources.\\n\\nA middle-ground approach, in my view, would involve substantial government subsidies combined with income-contingent loan systems. Under this model, tuition is reduced significantly but not eliminated, and graduates repay costs proportionally to their earnings. Australia and the United Kingdom have implemented variations of this system with reasonable success, maintaining quality education while managing public costs.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while completely free university education is a noble goal, a balanced funding model that ensures accessibility without placing excessive burden on taxpayers represents the most practical path forward.","keyArguments":["social mobility promotion","economic competitiveness","taxpayer burden concerns","student motivation issues","income-contingent funding model"]},{"id":"ESSAY-005","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["business","globalization"],"prompt":"Multinational corporations have become increasingly powerful in the global economy. Some view them as engines of economic development that create jobs and spread technology, while others see them as entities that exploit workers and damage local cultures. What is your view?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Multinational corporations wield enormous influence over the global economy, and their impact is complex and multifaceted. I believe that while these corporations can drive positive development, their operations require robust regulation to prevent exploitation and cultural harm.\\n\\nOn the positive side, multinational corporations serve as powerful engines of economic development. They bring foreign direct investment to developing countries, creating employment opportunities and transferring valuable technology and management expertise. Workers gain skills that enhance their long-term employability, while local suppliers benefit from integration into global supply chains. Countries such as Vietnam, Bangladesh, and Mexico have experienced significant economic growth partly attributable to multinational investment.\\n\\nHowever, the darker side of corporate globalization cannot be ignored. Some multinationals have been documented paying poverty wages, maintaining unsafe working conditions, and opposing unionization efforts in countries with weak labor protections. Furthermore, the homogenizing influence of global brands can erode local cultures, replacing diverse traditions with standardized consumer experiences. The concentration of economic power in a handful of global corporations also raises concerns about market competition and democratic accountability.\\n\\nI believe the solution lies not in rejecting multinational investment but in establishing stronger international frameworks for corporate accountability. Governments, international organizations, and civil society must collaborate to enforce labor standards, environmental protections, and fair tax practices globally. Consumers also play a crucial role through their purchasing decisions, rewarding companies that demonstrate genuine commitment to ethical practices.\\n\\nIn conclusion, multinational corporations can contribute positively to global development, but only when their operations are constrained by strong regulatory frameworks and informed consumer pressure.","keyArguments":["job creation and investment","technology transfer benefits","worker exploitation risks","cultural homogenization","international regulatory framework"]},{"id":"ESSAY-006","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["culture","globalization"],"prompt":"As English becomes the dominant global language, many minority languages are disappearing at an alarming rate. Some people believe this linguistic homogenization is a natural and beneficial evolution, while others argue that we must actively preserve endangered languages. Discuss both perspectives and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The accelerating extinction of minority languages represents one of the most significant cultural challenges of our era. While some view linguistic consolidation as natural progress, I firmly believe that active preservation of endangered languages is essential for maintaining cultural diversity and human knowledge.\\n\\nThose who accept language homogenization argue that a common global language facilitates international communication, trade, and scientific collaboration. English proficiency opens doors to educational and economic opportunities, and a unified linguistic landscape arguably reduces misunderstandings and conflicts between nations. From this perspective, language evolution has always occurred throughout human history, and attempting to preserve languages artificially goes against natural social progression.\\n\\nHowever, I find the preservation argument far more compelling because languages encode irreplaceable cultural and intellectual heritage. Each language represents a unique worldview, containing specialized knowledge about local ecosystems, medicinal plants, and sustainable practices that have been developed over millennia. When a language dies, we lose not just words but entire systems of thought, oral histories, and cultural identities. The disappearance of linguistic diversity impoverishes humanity's collective heritage in ways we may not fully understand.\\n\\nPractical preservation efforts have demonstrated that language loss is not inevitable. Bilingual education programs, documentation projects, and community-led revitalization initiatives in places like New Zealand with Maori and Hawaii with Hawaiian have successfully reversed language decline. These efforts show that communities can maintain their linguistic heritage while also acquiring globally dominant languages for practical purposes.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while global communication benefits from shared languages, this should not come at the cost of linguistic diversity. Societies should pursue bilingual models that preserve heritage languages alongside global languages.","keyArguments":["global communication benefits","economic opportunity access","encoded cultural knowledge loss","unique worldview preservation","bilingual education model"]},{"id":"ESSAY-007","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["society","technology"],"prompt":"The widespread adoption of remote work has fundamentally changed how people approach their careers. Some believe remote work improves work-life balance and productivity, while others argue it reduces collaboration and blurs the boundary between professional and personal life. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The shift toward remote work represents one of the most significant workplace transformations in modern history. Having considered both perspectives, I believe remote work offers net positive benefits, but only when implemented with clear boundaries and intentional collaboration strategies.\\n\\nSupporters of remote work emphasize its positive impact on work-life balance and personal productivity. Eliminating commutes returns hours of time to employees each week, reducing stress and environmental impact. Many workers report higher productivity when freed from office distractions and given autonomy over their schedules. Additionally, remote work expands employment opportunities for people with disabilities, caregivers, and those living outside major urban centers, promoting greater workforce inclusion.\\n\\nHowever, critics raise valid concerns about collaboration and boundary erosion. Spontaneous interactions that spark creativity and innovation are harder to replicate in virtual environments. New employees may struggle to absorb organizational culture and build professional networks without in-person mentorship. Furthermore, the blurring of work and home boundaries can paradoxically lead to longer working hours and increased burnout, as employees feel pressure to demonstrate constant availability.\\n\\nIn my opinion, a hybrid model offers the best of both worlds. Organizations should establish core in-office days for team collaboration, mentoring, and cultural activities, while allowing remote work for focused individual tasks. Clear policies regarding availability hours, communication expectations, and home office support can mitigate boundary issues. Technology investments in collaboration tools and virtual team-building can bridge the gap between in-person and remote interaction.\\n\\nUltimately, the success of remote work depends on deliberate design rather than default implementation. Organizations that thoughtfully structure flexible work arrangements will reap the greatest benefits for both employees and business outcomes.","keyArguments":["improved work-life balance","increased personal productivity","reduced spontaneous collaboration","work-home boundary erosion","hybrid model with clear policies"]},{"id":"ESSAY-008","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["health","society"],"prompt":"Governments around the world are considering taxing sugary drinks and unhealthy foods to combat rising obesity rates. Is this an effective strategy for improving public health, or does it represent an overreach of government authority? Discuss your view.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The proposal to tax unhealthy foods as a public health measure raises important questions about the appropriate role of government in citizens' dietary choices. I believe that while such taxes can be a useful component of a broader health strategy, they should not be relied upon as a standalone solution.\\n\\nSugar taxes have demonstrated measurable success in several countries. Mexico's sugar-sweetened beverage tax, implemented in 2014, led to a significant reduction in sugary drink purchases, particularly among lower-income households. The United Kingdom's Soft Drinks Industry Levy prompted manufacturers to reformulate products with less sugar, benefiting consumers regardless of their purchasing decisions. These examples suggest that fiscal measures can effectively nudge consumer behavior toward healthier choices.\\n\\nHowever, critics rightly argue that such taxes are regressive, disproportionately affecting lower-income families who spend a larger portion of their income on food. Additionally, taxation alone fails to address the root causes of poor nutrition, including limited access to fresh food in underserved areas, inadequate nutrition education, and aggressive marketing of unhealthy products to children. Without complementary measures, food taxes risk being perceived as punitive rather than constructive.\\n\\nA comprehensive approach to public health would combine taxation with positive incentives and education. Revenue from sugar taxes should be directed toward subsidizing fresh produce, funding nutrition education programs, and improving food access in disadvantaged communities. Clear food labeling requirements, restrictions on junk food advertising to children, and public awareness campaigns can reinforce the impact of fiscal measures.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while taxing unhealthy foods is a legitimate public health tool, it must be part of an integrated strategy that addresses the complex socioeconomic factors underlying poor nutrition, rather than government overreach.","keyArguments":["proven tax effectiveness","reformulation incentives","regressive tax impact","root cause neglect","comprehensive health strategy"]},{"id":"ESSAY-009","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["technology","society"],"prompt":"Some people believe that the development of autonomous vehicles will revolutionize transportation, making roads safer and more efficient. Others are concerned about job losses for professional drivers and the ethical dilemmas posed by self-driving technology. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Autonomous vehicles represent a technological breakthrough with far-reaching implications for society. While the potential benefits are substantial, I believe a cautious and phased approach is necessary to address the legitimate concerns surrounding this technology.\\n\\nThe case for autonomous vehicles is compelling from a safety and efficiency standpoint. Human error contributes to over 90% of traffic accidents worldwide, and self-driving systems have the potential to dramatically reduce fatalities by eliminating impaired, distracted, and fatigued driving. Furthermore, autonomous vehicles can optimize traffic flow through vehicle-to-vehicle communication, reducing congestion and emissions. For elderly and disabled individuals who cannot drive, this technology promises unprecedented mobility and independence.\\n\\nHowever, the transition will not be without significant costs. Millions of professional drivers — including truck drivers, taxi operators, and delivery personnel — face potential displacement from their livelihoods. Without adequate retraining programs and social safety nets, this technological disruption could exacerbate economic inequality and social unrest. Additionally, autonomous vehicles must be programmed to make split-second ethical decisions in unavoidable accident scenarios, raising profound moral questions about how machines should value human lives.\\n\\nI advocate for a managed transition that prioritizes both innovation and social responsibility. Governments should invest in large-scale retraining programs for affected workers, develop clear regulatory frameworks for autonomous vehicle testing and deployment, and facilitate public dialogue about the ethical parameters of self-driving technology. The technology should be introduced first in controlled environments before widespread urban deployment.\\n\\nIn conclusion, autonomous vehicles offer tremendous promise, but realizing their benefits while minimizing harm requires proactive planning, robust regulation, and genuine commitment to supporting displaced workers.","keyArguments":["accident reduction potential","mobility for disabled and elderly","professional driver displacement","ethical programming dilemmas","managed phased transition"]},{"id":"ESSAY-010","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["education","society"],"prompt":"Some educational systems emphasize competition, ranking students against each other through standardized tests and class rankings. Others prioritize collaborative learning, focusing on teamwork and collective achievement. Which approach do you think better prepares students for the modern world?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The tension between competitive and collaborative educational approaches reflects deeper questions about what skills students need to thrive in the modern world. In my view, while both have their place, an educational system that prioritizes collaboration while incorporating healthy competition better serves students and society.\\n\\nCompetitive educational models have traditionally been praised for driving excellence and preparing students for merit-based advancement. Standardized testing and class rankings provide clear metrics for evaluating performance, helping universities and employers identify top talent. Competition can motivate students to push beyond their perceived limits and develop resilience and self-discipline, qualities valuable in any career path.\\n\\nHowever, excessive competition carries significant drawbacks that collaborative learning addresses more effectively. High-stakes ranking systems can create intense anxiety, discourage creativity, and penalize students who learn differently but possess equally valuable skills. More importantly, modern workplaces increasingly require teamwork, communication, and cross-functional collaboration. The complex problems facing society — from climate change to public health crises — demand collective solutions that no individual can achieve alone.\\n\\nA balanced educational approach would emphasize collaborative projects while maintaining individually accountable assessments. Students should learn to work effectively in diverse teams, develop emotional intelligence, and practice constructive feedback. At the same time, healthy competition through academic clubs, sports, and innovation challenges can channel competitive drive productively without defining students' entire educational experience by test scores.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while competition has a role in education, collaborative learning better develops the interpersonal skills, adaptability, and collective problem-solving abilities that define success in the interconnected modern world.","keyArguments":["competition drives excellence","clear performance metrics","anxiety and creativity suppression","workplace collaboration demands","balanced collaborative approach"]},{"id":"ESSAY-011","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["environment","business"],"prompt":"Economic growth and environmental protection are often seen as conflicting goals. Some argue that developing countries should prioritize economic development over environmental concerns, as wealthy nations did during their industrialization. To what extent do you agree with this view?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The perceived conflict between economic development and environmental protection presents a profound ethical and practical challenge for developing nations. While I understand the argument for prioritizing growth, I believe that developing countries must pursue both simultaneously, learning from the costly mistakes of industrialized nations.\\n\\nThose who advocate for growth-first policies point out that wealthy nations achieved their prosperity through industrialization that paid little attention to environmental consequences. It seems hypocritical to deny developing countries the same path to prosperity that lifted billions out of poverty in Europe, North America, and East Asia. Furthermore, impoverished populations facing immediate survival concerns cannot be expected to prioritize long-term environmental goals over pressing economic needs.\\n\\nHowever, the context today differs fundamentally from the historical circumstances of early industrializers. The cumulative environmental damage from past industrialization has already pushed planetary systems to critical thresholds, and continuing down the same path would produce catastrophic consequences affecting developing nations most severely. Moreover, the cost of renewable energy and sustainable technologies has fallen dramatically, making green development increasingly economically viable.\\n\\nDeveloping countries can pursue a development model that achieves economic growth while protecting environmental resources. Investments in renewable energy infrastructure, sustainable agriculture, and green manufacturing can create jobs and attract international investment. International mechanisms such as green climate funds and technology transfer agreements can support this transition, with wealthy nations bearing a fair share of the cost given their historical emissions.\\n\\nIn conclusion, developing countries need not choose between economic prosperity and environmental stewardship. With appropriate international support and access to modern sustainable technologies, they can forge a new development path that avoids the environmental damage of previous industrialization.","keyArguments":["historical hypocrisy concern","immediate poverty priorities","changed global context","falling green technology costs","sustainable development alternatives"]},{"id":"ESSAY-012","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["culture","technology"],"prompt":"Streaming services and digital media have made entertainment more accessible than ever before. However, some people argue that this convenience has led to a decline in the quality of content and a loss of shared cultural experiences. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The digital streaming revolution has transformed how we consume entertainment, bringing both unprecedented access and unintended cultural consequences. I believe that while streaming has genuinely democratized content creation and access, it has also fragmented our shared cultural experiences in concerning ways.\\n\\nThe benefits of streaming are substantial and widely appreciated. Viewers can access vast libraries of content from around the world at affordable prices, discovering films, series, and music that would never have reached them through traditional distribution channels. Independent creators and international artists now have platforms to reach global audiences without gatekeeper approval. This democratization has enriched our cultural landscape with diverse voices and perspectives that traditional media often overlooked.\\n\\nHowever, the shift to streaming has genuine drawbacks that deserve consideration. The algorithm-driven recommendation culture tends to keep viewers within narrow content bubbles, reducing exposure to challenging or unexpected material. The emphasis on quantity and engagement metrics has arguably led to formulaic content designed for passive consumption rather than artistic ambition. Perhaps most significantly, the fragmentation of audiences across countless platforms means fewer shared cultural moments — the collective experience of watching the same program at the same time and discussing it the next day has largely disappeared.\\n\\nA nuanced perspective acknowledges both developments. Streaming has not eliminated quality content — outstanding productions continue to emerge — but the sheer volume of available material makes quality harder to discover. Similarly, while traditional shared viewing has declined, new forms of digital cultural community have emerged through social media discussions and fan communities.\\n\\nIn conclusion, streaming services have made entertainment more accessible and diverse, but we should be mindful of preserving spaces for quality content and shared cultural experiences.","keyArguments":["unprecedented content access","democratized creator opportunities","algorithm-driven content bubbles","declining shared experiences","new digital communities"]},{"id":"ESSAY-013","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["health","society"],"prompt":"The legalization of cannabis for both medical and recreational use has been a topic of intense debate in many countries. Some argue it brings medical benefits and reduces criminal activity, while others warn of public health risks and social costs. What is your position on this issue?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The debate over cannabis legalization encompasses complex medical, social, and criminal justice considerations. After examining the evidence, I support controlled legalization with strict regulatory frameworks that prioritize public health and social equity.\\n\\nThe medical case for cannabis legalization is increasingly well-supported by scientific evidence. Cannabinoid-based medications have demonstrated effectiveness in treating chronic pain, reducing chemotherapy-induced nausea, and managing certain neurological conditions. Denying patients access to potentially beneficial treatments on prohibitionist grounds raises serious ethical concerns. Furthermore, legalization allows for rigorous quality control, standardized dosing, and proper medical supervision that are impossible in illegal markets.\\n\\nFrom a criminal justice perspective, prohibition has proven both costly and counterproductive. Cannabis-related arrests have disproportionately affected minority communities, perpetuating cycles of disadvantage without reducing drug availability. Legalization redirects law enforcement resources toward more serious crimes while eliminating the violent illegal market that prohibition creates. Tax revenue from legal cannabis sales can fund education, addiction treatment, and community development programs.\\n\\nHowever, the public health concerns are genuine and require careful management. Heavy cannabis use, particularly among adolescents whose brains are still developing, is associated with cognitive impairment and increased risk of mental health disorders. Impaired driving and workplace safety issues must be addressed through clear regulations and enforcement. These risks, however, can be managed through evidence-based policies including age restrictions, potency limits, public education campaigns, and strict marketing regulations similar to those governing alcohol and tobacco.\\n\\nIn conclusion, the balanced approach is not prohibition nor unrestricted access, but rather a carefully regulated system that maximizes benefits while minimizing harms through public health-centered policies.","keyArguments":["medical efficacy evidence","criminal justice reform","quality control benefits","adolescent health risks","regulated public health framework"]},{"id":"ESSAY-014","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["business","society"],"prompt":"Some people believe that corporate social responsibility (CSR) should be a fundamental obligation of all businesses, while others argue that the primary responsibility of a company is to maximize profits for its shareholders. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The debate over corporate social responsibility reflects fundamentally different conceptions of the role of business in society. I believe that in the modern context, CSR is not just an ethical preference but a strategic necessity for long-term business success.\\n\\nThe shareholder primacy view, famously articulated by economist Milton Friedman, argues that businesses serve society best by focusing on profit maximization within the bounds of law and ethical custom. Companies that generate profits create jobs, pay taxes, produce useful goods, and provide returns to investors who may include ordinary people's pension funds. From this perspective, diverting resources to social causes misappropriates shareholder funds and imposes an unofficial tax on business without democratic accountability.\\n\\nHowever, the CSR perspective recognizes that businesses operate within a social ecosystem that they both depend upon and affect. Companies that ignore environmental impact, exploit workers, or damage communities ultimately face reputational damage, regulatory action, and consumer boycotts that harm their bottom line. By contrast, businesses that invest in sustainable practices, fair labor conditions, and community development often enjoy stronger customer loyalty, better employee retention, and enhanced brand value.\\n\\nThe most compelling evidence supports a strategic CSR approach. Companies like Unilever and Patagonia have demonstrated that sustainability commitments can coexist with strong financial performance when integrated into core business strategy rather than treated as peripheral charity. Employees increasingly seek employers whose values align with their own, and consumers are more willing to support brands they perceive as responsible corporate citizens.\\n\\nIn my opinion, the false dichotomy between profit and social responsibility has been outdated by changing stakeholder expectations. Modern businesses should view CSR as an investment in long-term resilience and competitiveness rather than a cost to be minimized.","keyArguments":["shareholder primacy model","profit-driven social benefit","social ecosystem dependence","strategic CSR value","changing stakeholder expectations"]},{"id":"ESSAY-015","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["globalization","culture"],"prompt":"International tourism has grown dramatically in recent decades. While it brings economic benefits to host countries, it can also damage local environments and cultures. To what extent do you think the benefits of international tourism outweigh its drawbacks?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"International tourism has become one of the world's largest industries, generating both substantial economic benefits and significant social and environmental costs. I believe that while tourism's advantages are considerable, they only outweigh its drawbacks when managed through sustainable practices and community-centered policies.\\n\\nThe economic case for tourism is powerful, particularly for developing nations with limited export alternatives. Tourism generates foreign currency earnings, creates employment across multiple sectors from hospitality to transportation, and stimulates infrastructure development that benefits local populations. In countries such as Thailand, tourism accounts for a significant portion of GDP and has lifted many communities out of poverty. Well-managed tourism can also incentivize the preservation of cultural heritage and natural environments that might otherwise face degradation.\\n\\nHowever, uncontrolled tourism development imposes severe costs that cannot be ignored. Popular destinations such as Venice, Barcelona, and Machu Picchu have experienced overtourism that degrades the very attractions visitors come to see. Local residents in many tourist destinations face rising housing costs driven by short-term rental platforms, displacement from their communities, and the erosion of authentic cultural practices transformed into commercial performances. Environmentally, mass tourism contributes to carbon emissions, waste management crises, and ecosystem damage in sensitive areas.\\n\\nThe key variable determining whether tourism benefits or harms a destination is governance. Countries and cities that implement visitor caps, sustainable infrastructure, community benefit-sharing mechanisms, and cultural protection measures can harness tourism's economic power while mitigating its negative impacts. Costa Rica's ecotourism model and Bhutan's high-value, low-impact tourism policy demonstrate that alternative approaches are viable.\\n\\nIn conclusion, tourism's benefits can outweigh its drawbacks, but only with deliberate policies that prioritize long-term sustainability over short-term profit.","keyArguments":["economic development catalyst","cultural preservation incentive","overtourism destination damage","local community displacement","sustainable tourism governance"]},{"id":"ESSAY-016","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["technology","health"],"prompt":"Social media platforms have been increasingly linked to mental health issues, particularly among young people. Some advocate for stricter regulation of these platforms, while others emphasize personal responsibility and digital literacy education. Which approach do you find more effective and why?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The relationship between social media and youth mental health has emerged as a defining public health challenge of the digital age. I believe that neither regulation nor personal responsibility alone is sufficient, and that an effective response requires combining both approaches in a complementary framework.\\n\\nThe case for regulation is grounded in the recognition that social media platforms are designed to maximize engagement through mechanisms that exploit psychological vulnerabilities. Infinite scroll features, variable reward systems, and algorithmically curated content are deliberately engineered to keep users engaged as long as possible, with young developing minds particularly susceptible to these design patterns. Just as societies regulate food safety, automobile design, and tobacco advertising to protect public health, digital platforms warrant regulatory oversight to ensure their products do not cause foreseeable harm.\\n\\nHowever, regulation has inherent limitations that make personal responsibility and education essential complements. Regulations move slowly while technology evolves rapidly, creating perpetual gaps between rules and reality. Furthermore, overly restrictive regulation could infringe on freedom of expression and limit the genuine benefits young people derive from online communities, particularly those who find social support unavailable in their physical environments.\\n\\nDigital literacy education empowers young people to navigate online spaces critically and intentionally. Teaching students to recognize manipulation tactics, manage screen time, evaluate information credibility, and maintain healthy online-offline balance develops lasting skills that regulation alone cannot provide. Parents and schools must partner in this educational effort.\\n\\nMy preferred approach combines thoughtful regulation targeting platform design practices with comprehensive digital literacy education. This dual strategy addresses both the supply side (platform accountability) and the demand side (user capability), creating an ecosystem where technology serves human well-being rather than exploiting human psychology for profit.","keyArguments":["platform design manipulation","exploitation of psychology","regulatory speed limitations","digital literacy empowerment","combined regulatory-educational approach"]},{"id":"ESSAY-017","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["education","globalization"],"prompt":"Some countries are increasingly promoting study abroad programs for university students, believing international exposure is essential for a well-rounded education. Others argue that the high costs of such programs make them accessible only to privileged students, exacerbating inequality. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Study abroad programs offer transformative educational experiences, yet their accessibility challenges raise legitimate concerns about equity in higher education. I believe international education should be expanded and democratized rather than restricted, with deliberate policies to ensure broad participation.\\n\\nInternational study experiences provide uniquely valuable educational outcomes that classroom learning alone cannot replicate. Students develop intercultural competence, foreign language proficiency, and adaptability through immersion in unfamiliar environments. These soft skills are increasingly valued by employers in a globalized economy. Furthermore, international academic exposure broadens intellectual horizons by exposing students to different scholarly traditions, research methodologies, and problem-solving approaches.\\n\\nHowever, the equity critique cannot be dismissed. Traditional semester-abroad programs carry substantial costs including tuition differentials, travel expenses, housing, and living costs that exclude students from lower-income backgrounds. When these valuable developmental experiences are systematically available only to the already privileged, they reinforce rather than reduce social inequality. This dynamic is particularly problematic at public universities with explicit missions to serve as engines of social mobility.\\n\\nI advocate for institutional reforms that expand international educational access. Universities should develop scholarship programs specifically for study abroad, using needs-based funding models. Short-term faculty-led programs can reduce costs while still providing meaningful international exposure. Virtual exchange programs using collaborative online learning have emerged as innovative alternatives that develop intercultural skills at minimal expense. Additionally, universities should build partnerships with institutions in lower-cost countries and regions to diversify affordable options.\\n\\nIn conclusion, rather than abandoning study abroad as elitist, we should work to democratize international education. The goal should be making global learning opportunities available to all students, regardless of their economic background.","keyArguments":["intercultural competence development","global employability skills","financial accessibility barriers","inequality reinforcement","democratization through institutional reform"]},{"id":"ESSAY-018","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["environment","business"],"prompt":"The concept of a circular economy, where waste is minimized and resources are reused, has gained traction as an alternative to the traditional linear economy. Do you think the circular economy is a realistic goal for modern societies, or is it an idealistic concept that cannot be practically implemented at scale?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The circular economy represents a compelling vision for sustainable development, but its practical feasibility has been subject to intense debate. I believe that while a fully circular economy may be an aspirational target, substantial progress toward circular principles is both realistic and urgently necessary.\\n\\nThe circular economy concept offers a coherent framework for addressing the environmental crisis created by our current linear take-make-dispose model. By designing products for durability, repairability, and recyclability, and by creating systems for material recovery and reuse, we can dramatically reduce resource extraction and waste generation. Companies like Philips have adopted circular business models, offering lighting as a service rather than selling bulbs, which incentivizes them to design longer-lasting, more efficient products.\\n\\nSignificant barriers nevertheless stand in the way of full implementation. Many modern products contain complex composite materials that are technically difficult and economically unviable to separate and recycle. Global supply chains are optimized for linear production, and transitioning to circular models requires massive infrastructure investment. Furthermore, consumer behavior and cultural norms centered on convenience and disposability present substantial behavioral challenges.\\n\\nRecognizing these obstacles does not mean abandoning circular principles. Progressive implementation in sectors with the highest impact potential can generate meaningful environmental benefits. The electronics industry can standardize components for easier repair, the construction sector can design buildings for material recovery, and food systems can convert organic waste into valuable compost and biogas. Policy instruments including extended producer responsibility laws, right-to-repair legislation, and landfill taxes can accelerate this transition.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while achieving a perfectly circular economy may be unrealistic in the near term, pursuing circular principles aggressively in high-impact sectors is not only feasible but essential. The question should not be whether the circular economy is perfectly achievable, but how much progress we can make toward it.","keyArguments":["resource depletion solution","successful business models","technical recycling barriers","supply chain constraints","sector-specific progressive implementation"]},{"id":"ESSAY-019","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["society","culture"],"prompt":"In many countries, the traditional family structure is changing, with more single-parent households, same-sex parents, and people choosing not to have children. Some view these changes as a sign of social progress and individual freedom, while others see them as a threat to social stability. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The evolution of family structures reflects broader social transformations that provoke both celebration and concern. I believe that diverse family forms represent social progress toward greater individual freedom and inclusion, and that concerns about stability are better addressed through supportive policies than through resistance to change.\\n\\nThose who view changing family structures as progress emphasize the expansion of personal freedom and the recognition of diverse life paths. The ability to leave unhappy or abusive marriages, the acceptance of same-sex relationships, and the choice to remain childfree all represent liberation from rigid social expectations that previously constrained individual fulfillment. Children raised in loving, stable households — regardless of family configuration — tend to thrive, and research increasingly shows that parenting quality matters far more than family structure.\\n\\nCritics, however, raise concerns that deserve thoughtful consideration rather than dismissal. Traditional family structures have historically served as primary units of economic support, elder care, and child socialization. When these structures weaken without adequate institutional alternatives, vulnerable individuals may fall through gaps in social support. Rising loneliness among elderly people without family networks and the challenges faced by single parents without adequate support systems represent real social costs.\\n\\nThe constructive response to changing family structures is not to resist diversity but to adapt social institutions accordingly. Flexible work policies that accommodate diverse caregiving arrangements, affordable childcare, paid parental leave for all family types, and robust social security systems can support well-being regardless of household composition. Nordic countries demonstrate that progressive family policies can coexist with diverse family forms while maintaining social cohesion.\\n\\nIn conclusion, diverse family structures represent genuine social progress in expanding human freedom and dignity. The appropriate policy response is to strengthen the social safety net to support all families, whatever form they take.","keyArguments":["personal freedom expansion","parenting quality over structure","social support gaps","elderly loneliness concerns","adaptive institutional policies"]},{"id":"ESSAY-020","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["technology","business"],"prompt":"The rapid advancement of automation and artificial intelligence is predicted to eliminate millions of jobs across various industries. Some people believe governments should implement universal basic income (UBI) as a solution, while others argue that history shows new jobs will emerge to replace those lost. Discuss both perspectives and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The prospect of widespread technological unemployment has placed universal basic income at the center of policy debates about the future of work. While both perspectives have merit, I believe we need proactive experimentation with income support mechanisms rather than relying solely on historical patterns or committing prematurely to full UBI.\\n\\nThe UBI argument draws strength from the unprecedented nature of current technological disruption. Unlike previous industrial revolutions that primarily replaced physical labor, artificial intelligence now threatens cognitive work across professional, creative, and analytical domains. If this wave of automation permanently reduces the demand for human labor, society needs new mechanisms for distributing purchasing power. UBI would provide economic security, enable retraining and entrepreneurship, and recognize unpaid contributions such as caregiving and community work.\\n\\nHistorical optimists counter that every technological revolution has ultimately created more jobs than it destroyed. The Industrial Revolution eliminated many agricultural and artisanal jobs but generated entirely new industries and professions unimaginable to previous generations. Human needs appear unlimited, and new technologies historically create new demands for human skills in designing, maintaining, and complementing automated systems.\\n\\nI find both positions partially persuasive but insufficient individually. The historical pattern offers genuine reassurance but cannot guarantee future outcomes given the qualitatively different nature of AI-driven automation. Meanwhile, implementing full UBI without understanding its effects on work incentives, inflation, and social cohesion carries significant risks.\\n\\nThe prudent path forward involves scaling up pilot programs to gather empirical evidence about UBI's effects across different contexts. Finland, Kenya, and several other countries have conducted promising experiments, but more research is needed. Simultaneously, investments in education reform, lifelong learning, and portable benefits systems can prepare workers for uncertain transitions regardless of whether UBI is ultimately adopted.\\n\\nIn conclusion, while the automation challenge is real, the most responsible response is evidence-based policy experimentation rather than ideological commitment to any single solution.","keyArguments":["unprecedented cognitive automation","UBI economic security rationale","historical job creation patterns","qualitative disruption differences","evidence-based policy experimentation"]},{"id":"ESSAY-021","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["technology","society"],"prompt":"Some people believe that children under 16 should not be allowed to have social media accounts. Others argue that social media is an important tool for young people to socialize and learn. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The debate about restricting social media access for minors pits concerns about mental health against the value of digital socialization. I believe age-appropriate restrictions combined with digital literacy education represent the most balanced approach.\\n\\nSocial media platforms present genuine risks to young users including cyberbullying, exposure to inappropriate content, and addictive design features that exploit developing brains. Studies have linked heavy social media use among adolescents to increased anxiety, depression, and poor body image. The algorithms powering these platforms are specifically designed to maximize engagement, making it difficult for even motivated young users to self-regulate their usage.\\n\\nHowever, social media also provides valuable opportunities for connection, self-expression, and learning that are particularly important for teenagers navigating identity formation. Young people use these platforms to maintain friendships, explore interests, and access educational content unavailable through traditional channels. Banning access entirely would isolate teenagers from their peers.\\n\\nA more nuanced approach would implement age-appropriate access levels with stronger parental controls, mandatory digital literacy education in schools, and platform accountability for protecting young users. This balanced strategy acknowledges both the genuine risks and legitimate benefits of young people's social media engagement while equipping them with the skills to navigate digital environments responsibly.","keyArguments":["mental health risks","cyberbullying and exposure","digital literacy education","social connection benefits","age-appropriate restrictions"]},{"id":"ESSAY-022","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["environment","business"],"prompt":"Electric vehicles are increasingly promoted as the solution to reducing transportation emissions. However, some critics argue that the environmental costs of battery production and electricity generation make them less green than advertised. What is your view on the environmental credentials of electric vehicles?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The environmental impact of electric vehicles is a nuanced issue requiring consideration of the full production and operational lifecycle. I believe EVs represent a significant environmental improvement over internal combustion engines, though they are not without environmental costs that require ongoing attention.\\n\\nThe lifecycle analysis of EVs consistently shows lower overall emissions than conventional vehicles, even when accounting for battery production and electricity generation from current grid mixes. As electricity grids continue incorporating more renewable sources, the operational advantage of EVs will increase further.\\n\\nHowever, the mining of lithium, cobalt, and rare earth minerals for batteries raises legitimate environmental and human rights concerns in producing regions. Battery production is energy-intensive and generates significant carbon emissions before the vehicle drives its first mile. The disposal and recycling infrastructure for EV batteries remains underdeveloped.\\n\\nThe solution is not to abandon the EV transition but to invest aggressively in sustainable mining practices, battery recycling technology, and renewable electricity generation. Governments and manufacturers must address the environmental costs of battery production while accelerating the clean energy transition that makes EVs progressively cleaner. Dismissing EVs because they are not perfectly clean would be far more environmentally damaging than pursuing their continued improvement.","keyArguments":["lower lifecycle emissions","battery production impact","mineral mining concerns","grid decarbonization","recycling infrastructure development"]},{"id":"ESSAY-023","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["education","technology"],"prompt":"With the rise of artificial intelligence tools that can write essays and solve problems, some argue that traditional homework is becoming obsolete. Others believe homework remains essential for developing discipline and independent learning skills. Discuss both perspectives and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The emergence of AI tools capable of completing academic assignments challenges traditional homework models but does not render them obsolete. I believe homework must evolve rather than disappear, focusing on tasks that develop skills AI cannot replicate.\\n\\nAI tools can indeed produce essays, solve math problems, and answer factual questions in seconds, making traditional take-home assignments vulnerable to academic dishonesty. If students can generate acceptable work without engaging with the material, the learning purpose of homework is undermined.\\n\\nHowever, homework serves purposes beyond content mastery that AI cannot replace. Regular independent practice develops self-discipline, time management, and persistence through challenging material. The process of struggling with difficult concepts builds cognitive resilience that instant AI assistance would short-circuit.\\n\\nThe solution lies in redesigning homework to emphasize process over product, incorporating in-class discussions of AI-assisted work, assigning tasks requiring personal reflection and original analysis, and using AI literacy as an explicit learning objective. Homework should teach students when and how to appropriately use AI tools rather than pretending they do not exist.","keyArguments":["AI-enabled academic dishonesty","self-discipline and perseverance","process over product","AI literacy integration","personal reflection requirements"]},{"id":"ESSAY-024","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["health","policy"],"prompt":"Should governments require mandatory vaccinations for all citizens, or should vaccination remain a matter of personal choice? Discuss the ethical, medical, and social implications of mandatory vaccination policies.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Mandatory vaccination policies raise fundamental tensions between individual autonomy and collective public health protection. I believe a nuanced approach requiring vaccination for certain activities while respecting conscientious objection where feasible best balances these competing values.\\n\\nThe public health case for widespread vaccination is scientifically robust. Vaccines have eradicated smallpox, dramatically reduced polio incidence, and prevented millions of deaths. Herd immunity protects vulnerable individuals who cannot receive vaccines for medical reasons, making vaccination a collective responsibility.\\n\\nHowever, medical ethics strongly values informed consent and bodily autonomy. Forced medical procedures violate fundamental human dignity principles and can erode trust in healthcare institutions. Historical medical abuses against marginalized communities contribute to legitimate vaccine hesitancy that coercion would exacerbate.\\n\\nA tiered approach offers a pragmatic middle ground: requiring vaccination for school enrollment and certain occupations while providing medical and conscientious exemptions, investing in transparent public education about vaccine safety, and addressing underlying healthcare access inequities. This balances public health imperatives with respect for individual autonomy and builds trust rather than compelling compliance.","keyArguments":["herd immunity protection","informed consent principle","historical medical trust issues","tiered requirement approach","public education investment"]},{"id":"ESSAY-025","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["business","globalization"],"prompt":"Cryptocurrencies and decentralized finance have been described as either the future of money or a speculative bubble that threatens financial stability. What is your assessment of the potential and risks of decentralized digital currencies?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Cryptocurrencies represent a genuine technological innovation with transformative potential, but their current manifestations exhibit significant risks that warrant cautious regulatory engagement rather than either uncritical embrace or outright prohibition.\\n\\nThe underlying blockchain technology enables peer-to-peer value transfer without centralized intermediaries, potentially reducing transaction costs, increasing financial inclusion for the unbanked, and providing alternatives in countries with unstable currencies. Smart contract platforms extend these capabilities to programmable financial instruments and decentralized governance.\\n\\nHowever, the current cryptocurrency ecosystem exhibits characteristics that justify substantial skepticism. Extreme price volatility makes cryptocurrencies unsuitable as stable stores of value. The sector has been plagued by fraud, hacking, and market manipulation. The environmental cost of proof-of-work mining is substantial. Pseudonymous transactions facilitate money laundering and ransomware.\\n\\nResponsible integration requires proportionate regulation addressing consumer protection, anti-money laundering compliance, and financial stability oversight while preserving space for innovation. Central bank digital currencies represent a middle path, combining the efficiency benefits of digital currency with the stability of traditional monetary systems.","keyArguments":["financial inclusion potential","blockchain technology benefits","price volatility risks","fraud and manipulation concerns","central bank digital currencies"]},{"id":"ESSAY-026","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["urban","environment"],"prompt":"Many cities around the world are introducing car-free zones in their city centers. Supporters believe this improves air quality and quality of life, while opponents argue it harms businesses and inconveniences residents. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The pedestrianization of city centers represents a significant shift in urban planning philosophy that generates both enthusiasm and opposition. I believe that well-planned car-free zones deliver substantial net benefits, though implementation details determine whether the transition succeeds or fails.\\n\\nThe case for car-free zones is compelling. Removing vehicles from city centers dramatically reduces air pollution and noise levels. Cities including Copenhagen, Barcelona, and Paris have demonstrated that pedestrianized areas attract more visitors who spend more time and money than drivers passing through. The reclaimed street space enables outdoor dining, public events, and children's play areas.\\n\\nHowever, opposition based on business impact and accessibility cannot be dismissed. Retailers dependent on customers carrying heavy purchases have legitimate concerns. Disabled and elderly residents who rely on car access face genuine mobility challenges that poorly designed car-free schemes can exacerbate.\\n\\nThe successful transition lies in phasing implementation with extensive stakeholder consultation, providing robust alternatives including improved transit, delivery hubs, and accessible parking at zone peripheries. Exemptions for disabled residents, emergency vehicles, and essential deliveries maintain necessary access. Cities that have implemented these transitional measures have generally seen initial opposition give way to broad support.","keyArguments":["air quality improvement","increased pedestrian spending","business accessibility concerns","disabled and elderly mobility","phased implementation with alternatives"]},{"id":"ESSAY-027","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["ethics","technology"],"prompt":"The development of lethal autonomous weapons systems, sometimes called killer robots, raises profound ethical questions. Some argue these systems should be banned preemptively, while others believe they could make warfare more precise and reduce civilian casualties. What is your position?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Autonomous weapons systems represent a moral threshold that humanity should not cross. I support a preemptive international ban on lethal autonomous weapons because the risks they pose to human dignity, international stability, and civilian protection far outweigh any potential military benefits.\\n\\nThe argument that autonomous weapons could reduce civilian casualties by eliminating human error overlooks fundamental realities. Human judgment, for all its flaws, can exercise compassion, recognize surrender, and understand contextual nuances that algorithms cannot replicate. An autonomous system cannot comprehend a child holding a toy versus a combatant holding a weapon.\\n\\nThe strategic risks are severe. Lowering barriers to lethal force through automation would likely make armed conflict easier to initiate. The possibility of malfunction, hacking, or unintended escalation through autonomous systems interacting at machine speed raises existential risks. An arms race in autonomous weapons would produce catastrophic instability.\\n\\nFundamentally, the delegation of lethal decision-making to machines crosses a moral line. When a human being dies, there should be a human being making that decision who can be held accountable. Algorithms cannot exercise judgment, compassion, or accountability. A preemptive ban, while challenging to negotiate and verify, offers the only responsible path forward.","keyArguments":["human judgment irreplaceability","contextual understanding limitation","conflict initiation risk","algorithmic non-accountability","international law violation"]},{"id":"ESSAY-028","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["society","media"],"prompt":"The decline of local journalism has created news deserts where communities lack professional reporting on local government and civic affairs. Some propose government funding for local journalism, while others fear this would compromise journalistic independence. What solution do you support?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The collapse of local journalism represents a crisis for democratic accountability that demands innovative solutions. I support a mixed funding model combining public funding with structural firewalls protecting editorial independence, complemented by philanthropic and community-based alternatives.\\n\\nThe scale of the crisis makes purely market-based solutions inadequate. Thousands of local newspapers have closed entirely, and those that survive often operate with skeleton staffs. Studies show that communities losing local newspapers experience reduced voter turnout, increased government borrowing costs, and more polarized voting patterns.\\n\\nGovernment funding through models similar to public broadcasting offers a proven mechanism. The BBC, PBS, and other public media organizations demonstrate that government funding can coexist with editorial independence when protected by structural firewalls including multi-year funding commitments and independent governing boards.\\n\\nHowever, exclusive reliance on government funding creates genuine risks. The government is both funder and subject of coverage. A diverse funding ecosystem including philanthropic foundations, community ownership models, and member-supported nonprofit newsrooms provides resilience. The goal should be sustaining the watchdog function of journalism through whatever funding mechanisms preserve independence and accountability.","keyArguments":["market failure evidence","public broadcasting model","editorial independence firewalls","government-subject conflict","diverse funding ecosystem"]},{"id":"ESSAY-029","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["health","society"],"prompt":"In many countries, governments are implementing sugar taxes on soft drinks and unhealthy foods. Supporters say this improves public health, while critics argue it is regressive and limits personal choice. To what extent do you support sugar taxes?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Sugar taxes represent a legitimate public health tool that can effectively reduce consumption of unhealthy products, though they must be designed carefully to address legitimate concerns about regressivity and be part of a comprehensive health strategy.\\n\\nThe evidence supporting sugar taxes is substantial. Mexico's sugar-sweetened beverage tax reduced purchases of taxed beverages significantly, with larger effects among lower-income households who benefit most from reduced sugar consumption. The UK's Soft Drinks Industry Levy prompted manufacturers to reformulate products with less sugar, benefiting all consumers.\\n\\nThe regressivity concern deserves serious attention. Lower-income households spend a larger proportion of their income on food and beverages, making consumption taxes disproportionately affect them. However, this concern must be weighed against the equally regressive health impacts of diet-related diseases, which disproportionately burden disadvantaged communities.\\n\\nThe most effective approach combines sugar taxes with positive measures that address regressivity. Revenue should be directed toward subsidizing fresh produce, funding nutrition education in underserved communities, and improving food access in disadvantaged areas. This transforms a regressive tax into a progressive health investment. The goal is not to punish consumers but to create an environment where healthy choices are easier and more affordable for everyone.","keyArguments":["proven tax effectiveness","product reformulation incentive","regressive health outcomes tradeoff","revenue for health programs","comprehensive health strategy"]},{"id":"ESSAY-030","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["education","psychology"],"prompt":"Competition in education, through exams, rankings, and awards, is often seen as a way to motivate excellence. Others believe collaboration better prepares students for the modern world. Which approach do you think creates better educational outcomes?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The tension between competitive and collaborative educational approaches reflects deeper questions about what skills students need to thrive. In my view, while competition has a legitimate role, an educational system that prioritizes collaboration while incorporating healthy competition better serves both students and society.\\n\\nCompetitive educational models have traditionally been praised for driving excellence through clear performance metrics and merit-based advancement. Standardized testing provides objective data for evaluating student progress. Competition can motivate students to push beyond perceived limits, developing resilience and self-discipline that are genuinely valuable in many professional contexts.\\n\\nHowever, excessive competition carries significant drawbacks that collaborative learning addresses more effectively. High-stakes ranking systems create intense anxiety, can discourage intellectual risk-taking and creativity, and penalize students who learn differently. Modern workplaces increasingly require teamwork, cross-functional collaboration, and collective problem-solving skills that competitive classroom environments do not naturally develop.\\n\\nA balanced educational approach would embed collaborative projects as central learning experiences while maintaining individually accountable assessments. Students should learn to work effectively in diverse teams, develop emotional intelligence, and practice constructive feedback. The goal should be developing students who are both individually capable and collectively effective.","keyArguments":["competition drives measurable excellence","anxiety and reduced creativity","workplace collaboration demands","complex problem collective solutions","balanced collaborative approach"]},{"id":"ESSAY-031","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["technology","privacy"],"prompt":"Governments around the world are expanding their use of surveillance technologies, including facial recognition and data collection, citing security concerns. Critics argue this creates a surveillance state that threatens civil liberties. Where should the line be drawn?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The expansion of government surveillance capabilities presents a fundamental challenge to democratic societies. I believe surveillance must be limited by principles of proportionality, judicial oversight, and transparency.\\n\\nGovernments have a genuine responsibility to protect citizens from terrorism and organized crime. Surveillance technologies including facial recognition can assist in locating missing persons and identifying criminal suspects. In limited, targeted applications with proper oversight, these tools can serve legitimate public safety functions.\\n\\nHowever, the deployment of mass surveillance infrastructure fundamentally alters the relationship between citizens and the state. The knowledge that one's movements and communications may be monitored chills free expression and political dissent. Historical experience demonstrates that surveillance powers, once established, are rarely rolled back and are frequently expanded beyond original justifications.\\n\\nThe appropriate line requires several principles. Surveillance should be targeted at specific threats rather than deployed as general population monitoring. Judicial warrants should be required for intrusive surveillance, with independent oversight bodies reviewing programs. Technologies with documented demographic biases, such as facial recognition, should face heightened scrutiny. The burden of proof should rest on governments to demonstrate necessity and proportionality.","keyArguments":["proportionality principle","judicial oversight requirement","chilling effects on expression","surveillance power creep","biometric bias concerns"]},{"id":"ESSAY-032","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["science","ethics"],"prompt":"Genetic engineering of human embryos has the potential to eliminate hereditary diseases but also raises profound ethical concerns about designer babies and permanent changes to the human gene pool. Should we proceed with research into human germline editing?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Human germline editing represents one of the most profound ethical frontiers in science, with extraordinary therapeutic potential and deeply concerning implications. I support carefully regulated research into germline editing for preventing severe genetic diseases, while opposing applications for enhancement purposes.\\n\\nThe therapeutic case for germline editing is scientifically and morally serious. Some genetic diseases cause profound suffering and early death, and germline editing could prevent these conditions not just in an individual patient but in all their descendants. For families affected by devastating hereditary conditions, this technology offers a path to having healthy biological children.\\n\\nHowever, the risks and ethical concerns are substantial. The technology's safety remains uncertain, with off-target modifications potentially causing harm affecting all future generations. The distinction between therapy and enhancement is contested and blurry, raising concerns about genetic class stratification. Disability communities warn that eliminating genetic conditions could devalue lives of people living with those conditions.\\n\\nThe path forward requires robust international governance frameworks, transparent public deliberation, and a focus on serious disease prevention rather than enhancement. Research should proceed slowly with broad societal input, not driven solely by scientific capability or commercial interest.","keyArguments":["severe disease prevention","heritable safety concerns","therapy-enhancement boundary","disability community perspectives","international governance framework"]},{"id":"ESSAY-033","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["society","culture"],"prompt":"In many countries, traditional festivals and cultural practices are declining as younger generations adopt more globalized lifestyles. Some see this as a natural evolution, while others believe cultural traditions should be actively preserved. What is your view?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The tension between cultural evolution and preservation reflects genuine dilemmas about identity, community, and change. I believe that cultural traditions deserve active support rather than passive acceptance of their decline, though preservation should focus on enabling living traditions rather than creating static museum pieces.\\n\\nThose who view cultural change as natural evolution correctly observe that cultures have always transformed throughout history. Today's traditions were yesterday's innovations. Globalization has brought genuine benefits including exposure to diverse perspectives.\\n\\nHowever, this perspective underestimates the asymmetric nature of contemporary cultural change. The decline of local traditions results from structural forces including commercial media concentration, economic pressures toward homogenization, and the dominance of a few global languages. When traditions disappear, communities lose intergenerational connections, local knowledge systems, and sources of meaning and belonging.\\n\\nEffective preservation supports communities in maintaining and adapting traditions on their own terms. Government support for traditional arts education, community cultural centers, and festivals can create conditions where traditions remain vibrant. The goal should be ensuring that communities have genuine choices about which traditions to maintain, adapt, or release, rather than having those choices made by market forces.","keyArguments":["asymmetric cultural change","structural homogenization forces","intergenerational connection loss","community self-determination","living tradition support"]},{"id":"ESSAY-034","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["business","ethics"],"prompt":"Corporate social responsibility has become increasingly important, with companies expected to address environmental, social, and governance issues. Some argue this distracts from the primary purpose of business, which is generating profit for shareholders. What is your view?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Corporate social responsibility has evolved from voluntary philanthropy to an expected dimension of business strategy. I believe responsible business practices are not a distraction from shareholder value but an increasingly important component of long-term value creation.\\n\\nThe traditional shareholder primacy view argues that businesses serve society best by maximizing profits within legal bounds. Companies generate jobs, pay taxes, and provide returns to investors. Diverting resources to social causes arguably imposes an unofficial tax without democratic accountability.\\n\\nHowever, stakeholders including employees, customers, and communities increasingly expect companies to address their broader impacts. Those that ignore these expectations face talent acquisition difficulties, consumer boycotts, and regulatory action. Environmental degradation and labor exploitation create material risks to long-term business performance.\\n\\nCompanies including Unilever and Patagonia demonstrate that sustainability commitments can coexist with strong financial performance when integrated into core business strategy. The most effective approach integrates social responsibility into the business model rather than treating it as peripheral charity, recognizing that long-term shareholder value depends on maintaining social license to operate.","keyArguments":["stakeholder expectation shift","material risk management","talent and consumer consequences","integrated business model","social license to operate"]},{"id":"ESSAY-035","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["environment","development"],"prompt":"Developing countries argue they should have the right to use fossil fuels for economic growth as wealthy nations did during their industrialization. Others contend that all countries must transition to clean energy to address climate change. How should this tension be resolved?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The tension between development rights and climate responsibility represents one of the most challenging dimensions of international climate politics. I believe resolving this requires wealthy nations to provide substantial financial and technological support for clean energy transition in developing countries.\\n\\nThe developing country position carries significant moral weight. Wealthy nations achieved their prosperity through centuries of unrestricted fossil fuel use. It is fundamentally unjust to demand that developing countries forgo the development path that created prosperity elsewhere when hundreds of millions still lack access to reliable electricity.\\n\\nHowever, the global context has fundamentally changed. The cumulative environmental damage from past emissions has pushed planetary systems to critical thresholds, and developing countries will suffer disproportionately from climate impacts. Furthermore, the cost of renewable energy has fallen dramatically, making clean development increasingly economically competitive.\\n\\nThe resolution lies in wealthy nations fulfilling their climate finance commitments, providing technology transfer and capacity building. The annual 100 billion dollar climate finance commitment must be met and expanded. Debt relief, concessional financing for renewable infrastructure, and support for just transitions are essential. Developing countries should pursue low-carbon pathways as a supported transition serving their own long-term interests.","keyArguments":["historical emissions asymmetry","energy poverty alleviation","falling renewable costs","climate finance commitments","supported transition pathway"]},{"id":"ESSAY-036","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["education","policy"],"prompt":"Some countries are making university education free for all citizens. Supporters say this promotes equality, while critics argue it is too expensive and benefits the wealthy who are more likely to attend university. Which view is more convincing?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The debate over free university education involves genuine trade-offs between equality, cost, and incentive effects. I believe a progressive funding model with income-contingent repayment better balances access, fairness, and fiscal sustainability.\\n\\nThe case for free university education rests on compelling principles of equal opportunity. Higher education generates substantial positive externalities including a more productive workforce and greater civic engagement. When talented students are excluded by cost, society loses their potential contributions.\\n\\nHowever, the critique that universal free tuition disproportionately benefits the already advantaged has empirical support. University students disproportionately come from wealthier families, and universal subsidies therefore transfer resources from all taxpayers to a relatively privileged group. The opportunity cost in terms of other public investments like early childhood education is substantial.\\n\\nThe most convincing approach combines reduced financial barriers with income-contingent loan systems. Australia and the United Kingdom demonstrate that eliminating upfront costs while requiring graduates to repay based on earnings achieves access without regressive subsidy. Those who benefit most contribute more, while low earners are protected.","keyArguments":["equal opportunity principle","regressive subsidy evidence","opportunity cost consideration","income-contingent loan model","progressive funding design"]},{"id":"ESSAY-037","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["media","technology"],"prompt":"The rise of streaming platforms has fundamentally changed how we consume entertainment. Some celebrate the unprecedented access to content, while others lament the loss of shared cultural experiences. Do the benefits of streaming outweigh its drawbacks?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The streaming revolution has transformed entertainment consumption with both liberating and fragmenting effects. I believe streaming's benefits substantially outweigh its drawbacks, though the losses to shared cultural experience deserve acknowledgment.\\n\\nStreaming's advantages are transformative. Viewers access vast libraries of content from around the world at historically low cost. Independent creators reach global audiences without gatekeeper approval. This democratization has enriched our cultural landscape with diverse voices and perspectives that traditional media often overlooked.\\n\\nHowever, the fragmentation of audiences across countless platforms has genuine cultural costs. Fewer shared cultural moments occur when everyone watches different things at different times. Algorithmic curation can trap viewers in content bubbles, reducing exposure to challenging or unexpected material.\\n\\nThese losses do not outweigh the gains, but they require attention. New forms of shared cultural experience have emerged, including social media discussions and fan communities. The challenge is to intentionally create spaces for shared cultural experience within the abundant streaming landscape, recognizing the gains while addressing the fragmentation.","keyArguments":["unprecedented content access","creator democratization","algorithmic content bubbles","shared experience decline","new community formations"]},{"id":"ESSAY-038","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["philosophy","technology"],"prompt":"As AI systems become more sophisticated, some argue they could eventually deserve rights similar to human rights. Others consider this absurd, arguing that rights are inherently tied to human consciousness and experience. What is your view on the possibility of AI rights?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The question of AI rights forces us to examine the foundations of moral status. I believe that while current AI systems clearly lack the properties warranting rights, we should take seriously the possibility that future systems might cross ethically relevant thresholds.\\n\\nThe argument against AI rights rests on the nature of current AI systems. These systems lack subjective experience, consciousness, and capacity for suffering that ground moral status in sentient beings. Rights protect beings who can be harmed, and entities without phenomenal experience cannot be harmed in morally relevant ways.\\n\\nHowever, the confidence with which we dismiss AI consciousness should be tempered by humility about our understanding of consciousness. If consciousness is substrate-independent and emerges from sufficiently complex information processing, advanced AI systems could potentially develop conscious experience.\\n\\nThe prudent approach develops ethical frameworks sensitive to evidence of consciousness rather than assuming its presence or absence based on substrate. We should invest in research on machine consciousness and establish precautionary principles against creating systems that might suffer.","keyArguments":["consciousness threshold","subjective experience requirement","scientific uncertainty about consciousness","substrate-independence possibility","precautionary ethical framework"]},{"id":"ESSAY-039","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["work","society"],"prompt":"The four-day work week is gaining support in some countries as a way to improve work-life balance and productivity. Others worry it would harm economic competitiveness. Do you think a four-day work week should be widely adopted?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The four-day work week proposal challenges fundamental assumptions about productivity and work organization. I support its wider adoption through experimentation, as evidence from trials suggests it can benefit both workers and employers when implemented thoughtfully.\\n\\nThe case for the four-day work week draws on evidence from large-scale trials in Iceland, the United Kingdom, and New Zealand. These experiments have generally found that reducing working hours without reducing pay maintains or even improves productivity, as workers are better rested and more focused. Employee wellbeing improves substantially.\\n\\nHowever, concerns about economic competitiveness deserve consideration. Not all industries can compress work into fewer days without reducing output. Customer-facing businesses, healthcare, and education cannot simply close for an additional day. Transition costs are real and unevenly distributed.\\n\\nThe appropriate path is neither universal mandate nor dismissal. Sector-specific experimentation allows evidence to accumulate. The goal should be evidence-based evolution toward working patterns that maximize both human wellbeing and productive output, recognizing that the five-day week is a relatively recent historical development.","keyArguments":["productivity maintenance evidence","employee wellbeing improvement","sector-specific feasibility","global competitiveness concern","evidence-based experimentation"]},{"id":"ESSAY-040","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["globalization","culture"],"prompt":"The dominance of English as a global language facilitates international communication but contributes to the decline of minority languages. Should there be active efforts to preserve endangered languages, or is language extinction a natural process?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The accelerating extinction of languages represents a cultural and intellectual loss that warrants active preservation efforts. I believe the current rate and causes of language death justify deliberate intervention.\\n\\nLinguists estimate approximately forty percent of the world's seven thousand languages are endangered, with one dying approximately every two weeks. This rate far exceeds any natural baseline and is driven by economic pressures favoring dominant languages and educational systems excluding minority languages.\\n\\nWhat is lost extends beyond vocabulary. Languages encode unique cultural knowledge including traditional ecological knowledge about local environments and medicinal plants accumulated over millennia. They embody distinct ways of conceptualizing time, space, and kinship representing irreplaceable human intellectual heritage.\\n\\nSuccessful revitalization programs demonstrate that language decline is reversible. Maori in New Zealand and Hawaiian in Hawaii have been brought back through community-led immersion education and official recognition. Bilingualism is not transitional but a stable condition practiced by the majority of the world's population.","keyArguments":["unnatural extinction rate","encoded cultural knowledge loss","unique cognitive frameworks","successful revitalization examples","stable bilingualism model"]},{"id":"ESSAY-041","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["technology","work"],"prompt":"Automation and AI are predicted to eliminate millions of jobs across various industries. Some propose universal basic income as a solution, while others believe new jobs will emerge as they have in past technological revolutions. Which view is more realistic?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The prospect of widespread technological unemployment requires abandoning both utopian and dismissive extremes in favor of evidence-based policy preparation. I believe the historical pattern of job creation does not guarantee future outcomes given the qualitatively different nature of AI-driven automation.\\n\\nHistorical precedent provides genuine reassurance. Every major technological revolution eliminated specific jobs while creating entirely new categories of employment. Human desires appear unlimited, and new technologies have historically created demand for human skills.\\n\\nHowever, current automation differs qualitatively. AI now threatens cognitive and creative work across professional domains, not just manual labor. The speed of deployment and potential for AI to improve without limit challenge assumptions that new jobs will emerge faster than old ones disappear.\\n\\nThe prudent approach is neither premature commitment to UBI nor complacent reliance on history. Governments should fund pilot programs to gather evidence about UBI's effects. Simultaneously, investments in education reform, lifelong learning, and portable benefits systems prepare workers for uncertain transitions.","keyArguments":["historical job creation pattern","qualitative AI disruption difference","speed and breadth of impact","UBI pilot evidence gathering","lifelong learning infrastructure"]},{"id":"ESSAY-042","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["health","psychology"],"prompt":"Mental health awareness has increased significantly in recent years, with more people openly discussing depression, anxiety, and other conditions. Some argue this has led to over-medicalization of normal emotions, while others see it as reducing harmful stigma. What is your view?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The increase in mental health awareness represents genuine progress in reducing stigma and encouraging treatment-seeking, though concerns about over-medicalization deserve careful attention. I believe the net effect has been positive.\\n\\nThe destigmatization of mental health conditions has tangible benefits. Historically, mental illness was shrouded in shame preventing millions from seeking help. The growing willingness to discuss depression and anxiety has normalized treatment-seeking and increased funding for mental health services.\\n\\nHowever, the concern about over-medicalization is not baseless. The boundary between clinical conditions and normal emotional responses is inherently fuzzy. The expansion of diagnostic categories has arguably encouraged framing ordinary sadness and worry as medical problems.\\n\\nThe resolution lies in developing more nuanced public understanding. Mental health literacy should recognize that emotional distress exists on a spectrum. Social determinants including poverty and isolation should be recognized as upstream causes rather than framing mental health purely as biomedical conditions.","keyArguments":["stigma reduction benefits","treatment-seeking normalization","fuzzy diagnostic boundaries","pharmaceutical marketing concerns","nuanced public understanding"]},{"id":"ESSAY-043","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["transport","environment"],"prompt":"Some cities are investing heavily in bicycle infrastructure, creating dedicated bike lanes and bike-sharing programs. Supporters say this reduces traffic and pollution, while critics argue it inconveniences drivers and is impractical in many climates. Is investing in cycling infrastructure a good use of public funds?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Investment in cycling infrastructure represents one of the most cost-effective public expenditures available to cities, delivering outsized returns in public health, environmental quality, and urban livability. I believe cycling infrastructure deserves substantially increased investment.\\n\\nCopenhagen and Amsterdam demonstrate that comprehensive bike networks shift significant mode share from cars to bicycles, reducing traffic congestion and carbon emissions. The health benefits of regular cycling produce healthcare cost savings that alone justify infrastructure expenditure. Bike lanes are dramatically cheaper per kilometer than roads or rail.\\n\\nCritics' concerns about climate deserve acknowledgment. While cycling is less comfortable in extreme weather, cities with harsh winters including Oulu, Finland maintain high winter cycling rates through snow clearance and appropriate infrastructure. Protected bike lanes that physically separate cyclists from vehicle traffic address the primary barrier to cycling adoption: safety.\\n\\nContext-sensitive implementation is essential. The investment should be proportional to density, trip distances, and climate, with the highest returns in urban areas where short car trips are most replaceable.","keyArguments":["cost-effective infrastructure returns","health cost savings","successful winter cycling cities","mode shift rebalancing","context-sensitive implementation"]},{"id":"ESSAY-044","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["economics","society"],"prompt":"Wealth inequality has increased dramatically in many countries over recent decades. Some argue that inequality is a necessary incentive for innovation and economic growth, while others believe it undermines social cohesion and democratic governance. Should reducing inequality be a policy priority?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The dramatic increase in wealth inequality represents one of the defining economic challenges of our era. I believe reducing extreme inequality should be a policy priority, not through punitive redistribution but through reforms broadening opportunity and reducing concentration of power.\\n\\nThe argument that inequality incentivizes innovation contains partial truth. Financial reward motivates risk-taking and productive effort. Some inequality is inevitable and potentially functional in market economies, reflecting differences in effort and contribution.\\n\\nHowever, current inequality far exceeds what incentivizes productive effort and has become self-reinforcing through mechanisms undermining meritocracy. Extreme wealth concentration translates into disproportionate political influence through campaign contributions and lobbying. Intergenerational wealth transmission means birth circumstances increasingly determine life outcomes.\\n\\nPolicy responses should focus on widening opportunity rather than simply redistributing outcomes. Progressive taxation of wealth and inheritance, investment in early childhood education, strengthening collective bargaining, and enforcing competition policy all reduce inequality while supporting economic dynamism. The goal is outcomes reflecting effort and contribution rather than inherited advantage.","keyArguments":["partial incentive validity","self-reinforcing inequality mechanisms","political influence concentration","intergenerational opportunity gaps","opportunity-widening policies"]},{"id":"ESSAY-045","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["food","environment"],"prompt":"Plant-based diets are increasingly promoted for both health and environmental reasons. Some argue everyone should reduce meat consumption, while others believe dietary choices are a matter of personal freedom. Should governments encourage plant-based eating?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Governments have a legitimate role in encouraging plant-based eating patterns through the same policy tools applied to other public health priorities. I believe moderate government encouragement is appropriate given the significant public costs of current dietary patterns.\\n\\nThe public interest in dietary choices is well-established. Diet-related diseases impose enormous costs on healthcare systems. Livestock production contributes approximately fifteen percent of global greenhouse gas emissions and is a leading driver of deforestation and biodiversity loss.\\n\\nHowever, the means of influence must respect personal freedom and cultural diversity. Appropriate policy tools inform, incentivize, and enable rather than prohibit. Public education campaigns, nutritional guidelines reflecting sustainability considerations, and procurement policies increasing plant-based options in schools represent appropriate engagement.\\n\\nCultural sensitivity is essential. Dietary patterns are deeply embedded in traditions, religious practices, and social identities. Governments should promote flexible shifts toward more plant-forward eating rather than rigid requirements. Meat reduction rather than elimination represents a more effective and respectful approach than prescriptive mandates.","keyArguments":["public health cost justification","environmental externality principle","inform and incentivize approach","procurement policy tool","cultural sensitivity requirement"]},{"id":"ESSAY-046","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["privacy","technology"],"prompt":"As smart home devices become more common, they collect vast amounts of personal data about our daily lives. Some value the convenience these devices provide, while others worry about privacy and surveillance. Do the benefits of smart home technology outweigh the privacy risks?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Smart home technology offers genuine convenience and accessibility benefits, but current data collection practices are disproportionately invasive. I believe the technology's benefits could outweigh privacy risks with substantially stronger regulatory protections.\\n\\nSmart home devices provide meaningful value. Voice assistants help people with disabilities control their environments independently. Smart thermostats reduce energy consumption. For elderly people aging in place, these technologies extend independence and safety.\\n\\nHowever, the privacy implications are severe. Always-listening microphones and detailed movement tracking create comprehensive portraits of private life far beyond what is necessary for functionality. This data is valuable to advertisers, insurers, and law enforcement. Terms of service are typically opaque, and consumers lack meaningful choice.\\n\\nThe path forward requires regulation establishing clear principles. Data collection should be minimized to what is functionally necessary. Local processing should be preferred over cloud transmission. Consumers should have genuine opt-out rights for non-essential data collection. Strong security standards should be mandatory.","keyArguments":["accessibility and independence benefits","disproportionate data collection","opaque consent mechanisms","data minimization principle","regulatory protection requirements"]},{"id":"ESSAY-047","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":3,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["sports","society"],"prompt":"Professional athletes are often paid enormous salaries, while professions like teachers, nurses, and social workers earn comparatively little. Some see this as a reflection of market value, while others view it as a sign of misplaced societal priorities. What is your opinion?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The disparity between professional athlete salaries and those of essential service professionals reflects genuine market dynamics but also signals societal values deserving critical examination. I believe the disparity is less a problem to solve through wage controls and more a symptom of how we fund public goods.\\n\\nAthlete salaries reflect specific market conditions. Professional sports generate enormous revenue because hundreds of millions of people voluntarily choose to spend money and attention on entertainment. Athletes receive a share of the value they create within extremely competitive labor markets.\\n\\nHowever, the gap between entertainment and essential service compensation reflects market failures in valuing socially important work. Teaching, nursing, and social work produce enormous positive externalities whose benefits accrue broadly across society. Markets cannot easily monetize long-term benefits of an educated citizenry.\\n\\nThe appropriate response is not capping athlete salaries but increasing public investment in professions producing public goods. Higher teacher and nurse salaries through public funding would reflect genuine social value. The question should be why societies underinvest in fundamental professions, not why athletes earn so much.","keyArguments":["market revenue reflection","competitive labor markets","public good externalities","market valuation failure","public investment priority"]},{"id":"ESSAY-048","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["science","ethics"],"prompt":"Animal testing remains widely used in medical research despite ethical objections. Some argue it is necessary for developing life-saving treatments, while animal rights advocates believe it is morally unjustifiable regardless of benefits. Where do you stand on the use of animals in scientific research?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The use of animals in scientific research presents a genuine moral dilemma. I support a framework based on the three Rs: replacement wherever possible, reduction in numbers used, and refinement to minimize suffering.\\n\\nAnimal research has contributed substantially to medical advances. Vaccines, antibiotics, cancer treatments, and surgical techniques were developed through animal studies. The scientific consensus is that animal studies remain essential for understanding disease mechanisms and evaluating treatment safety.\\n\\nHowever, the moral status of animals demands that their use be treated as requiring justification. The capacity of research animals to experience pain, distress, and social needs creates genuine moral obligations. Trivial or duplicative research where alternatives could achieve equivalent results is ethically unacceptable.\\n\\nAdvances in organ-on-chip technologies, computer modeling, and cell culture systems are progressively reducing the need for animal testing. Regulatory frameworks should require researchers to demonstrate that alternatives are not available. Investment in alternative methods should increase substantially toward the long-term goal of eliminating animal testing.","keyArguments":["medical research contributions","animal capacity for suffering","proportional justification principle","alternative methods advancement","three Rs framework"]},{"id":"ESSAY-049","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":2,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["urban","society"],"prompt":"Many cities are facing severe housing affordability crises, with young people increasingly unable to afford homes. Some propose rent control as a solution, while others argue it discourages construction and ultimately worsens shortages. Which approach is more likely to address housing affordability?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"Housing affordability requires distinguishing between short-term tenant protection and long-term supply solutions. I believe the primary focus must be on dramatically increasing housing supply through zoning reform and public investment.\\n\\nThe economic consensus against rent control has strong empirical foundations. Strict rent caps reduce profitability of rental development, discouraging new construction and ultimately reducing supply. They can create lock-in effects reducing labor mobility. These effects are well-documented in cities maintaining strict controls.\\n\\nHowever, the complete dismissal of tenant protections is also problematic. Vulnerable tenants need protection from displacement in the interim. Moderate rent stabilization exempting new construction preserves development incentives while preventing predatory increases.\\n\\nThe primary solution must be supply expansion. Zoning reform allowing multi-family housing, streamlined permitting, and public investment in social housing represent the fundamental path to affordability. Tenant protections buy time for supply measures to take effect but cannot substitute for building enough housing.","keyArguments":["supply reduction evidence","lock-in effect concerns","moderate stabilization role","zoning reform priority","social housing investment"]},{"id":"ESSAY-050","type":"ESSAY","skill":"writing","difficulty":4,"timeLimit":1200,"instructions":"就以下话题写一篇200-300词的议论文。","tags":["philosophy","politics"],"prompt":"Freedom of speech is considered a fundamental right in democratic societies, but there is ongoing debate about its limits. Should speech that is offensive, harmful, or promotes hatred be restricted, or does protecting free expression require tolerating even deeply objectionable views?","minWords":200,"maxWords":300,"sampleAnswer":"The boundaries of free speech represent one of the most contested questions in democratic theory. I believe that while broad speech protections are fundamental to democracy, carefully defined restrictions on speech directly threatening violence or harassing individuals are compatible with democratic principles.\\n\\nThe case for robust speech protections rests on principled and pragmatic foundations. Democratic self-governance requires citizens to criticize those in power and access the full range of ideas for informed decision-making. Historically, speech restrictions have silenced dissent and protected established power more than vulnerable communities.\\n\\nHowever, absolute speech protections ignore that speech can cause genuine harm. Direct incitement to violence, targeted harassment silencing individuals through intimidation, and deliberate disinformation cause measurable damage. Purportedly neutral protections operate on an uneven playing field where those with power dominate public discourse.\\n\\nThe appropriate framework distinguishes based on nature and severity of harm. Direct threats and targeted harassment causing specific, identifiable harm are regulable. Speech expressing offensive or hateful opinions should generally be addressed through counter-speech, education, and social sanction rather than legal prohibition. This distinction, while imperfect, provides a workable principle for navigating between protection from harm and protection of expression.","keyArguments":["democratic self-governance requirement","historical censorship patterns","direct harm distinction","power asymmetry in speech","counter-speech over prohibition"]}]`);export{e as default};